# neosay

Display an 8-bit Neopet in your terminal with your own text. Same idea as `cowsay`/`ponysay`.

```
      ▄▄▄▄
    ▄██████▄      ╭────────────────────╮
   ████░░████  ───┤ Neopia awaits!     │
   ██████████     ╰────────────────────╯
    ▀██████▀
      ▀▀▀▀
```
(actual output is full 24-bit colour block art, not this sketch)

40 of the Neopets are supported, from Acara through Zafara.

## Pet storage

Pets live as `.rgbz` files (neosay's own image format. See "How pet
art is stored" below) under `~/.neosay/pets/`. Override root with the
`NEOSAY_HOME` env var. This is deliberately *outside* the project
folder, i.e. wherever you cloned or unzipped `neosay` itself (Downloads,
`~/dev`, etc.) and can be deleted after running `./install.sh`, and
your pets and rendered-art cache stay put.

Drop a file named `<slug>.rgbz` into that folder yourself (`acara.rgbz`,
`shoyru.rgbz`, etc. -- `neosay -l` shows every slug) and it's available
immediately, no reinstall needed.

## Install

```bash
git clone <this repo>
cd neosay
./install.sh                     # installs `neosay` + sets up pet storage
./install.sh path/to/pets.zip    # ...and imports pets you already have, in one go
```

`install.sh` uses `pip install --user .` when pip is available (handling
PEP 668 "externally managed" systems automatically), and falls back to a
small wrapper script in `~/.local/bin` if pip isn't present. Either way,
it makes sure that directory is on your `PATH` (adding a line to your
`~/.bashrc`/`~/.zshrc` if needed) so `neosay` works as a plain command from
any directory -- no `./` or `python3 -m` required. If it had to edit your
shell rc file, open a new terminal (or `source` it) once.

## Use

```bash
neosay "Neopia awaits!"
neosay -f shoyru "Wanna race to the Grundo Space Station?"
neosay -T "hmm, maybe an Ummagine Snowcone..."   # thought bubble
echo "night falls over Neopia" | neosay          # pipe input works
fortune | neosay                                 # classic ponysay move
neosay -l                                        # list pets + which you have
neosay --fetch                                   # neofetch-style system info in a bubble
```

Flags:

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `-f SLUG` | pick a specific pet (see `neosay -l` for slugs) |
| `-l` | list all 40 pets and which you have images for |
| `-T` | thought bubble instead of speech bubble |
| `-W N` | wrap the bubble text at N columns (default 40) |
| `--art-width N` | pet art width in terminal columns (default 34) |
| `--ascii` | plain ASCII box instead of Unicode box-drawing |
| `-F`, `--fetch` | print neofetch-style system info in the bubble |

With no `-f`, a random pet from whatever's in `data/` is used.

## How pet art is stored

Pets are stored in neosay's own `.rgbz` format instead of PNG: a small
header (magic bytes, version, width, height) followed by the raw RGBA
pixel data, zlib-compressed. PNG's chunk/filter machinery is overkill for
small, flat-coloured 8-bit sprites -- a flat header plus zlib compresses
these just as well with a far simpler format. It's lossless: decoding a
`.rgbz` file reproduces the exact pixels it was encoded from. The full
layout is documented at the top of `neosay/rgbz.py`.

## How it renders

Each pet image gets resized to a small grid and printed with the terminal
"half-block" trick (`▀`/`▄` glyphs with independent foreground/background
truecolor), so two source pixels become one character cell. Transparent
pixels are left as blank space so the art sits cleanly on your terminal's
own background. Rendered output is cached as plain text, so repeat runs
skip Pillow entirely.

Both the pet art and the render cache live under `~/.neosay/` (`pets/`
and `art/` respectively). Override the root with the `NEOSAY_HOME` env
var.

## Project layout

```
neosay/
  manifest.py       40 pet slugs -> display names (+ official URLs, for reference)
  rgbz.py           neosay's own image format: encode/decode
  render.py         .rgbz -> half-block ANSI art, with caching
  bubble.py         text wrapping + speech/thought bubble box drawing
  compose.py        lays the pet art and bubble out side by side
  cli.py            argument parsing / orchestration / pet storage paths
  data/             only the bundled data/_demo.rgbz ships here
import_assets.py    imports pets from a zip/folder into ~/.neosay/pets/
install.sh          sets up the `neosay` command, migrates + caches pet art
```

